      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Musculoskeletal injuries affect up to 13% of adults annually. Despite this high incidence, quality of primary care, including analgesia, may be sub-optimal. The goal of this study is to describe the quality of primary care for ambulatory patients with isolated limb injury and to identify related factors."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A cross sectional study was undertaken prospectively on 166 consecutive ambulatory adult patients with isolated limb injury who presented to orthopedics service in a Level one Trauma Centre. Quality of care was assessed by evaluating analgesia, walking aids, immobilization, and quality of referral diagnosis according to actual expert recommendations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "This study revealed low quality of primary care for 82 (53.2%) of injured patients. Seventy-three patients (50.3%) had pain level over 5/10 and 45 cases (28.5%) had insufficient/absent analgesia prescriptions. Fifty-one (32.3%) had unacceptable immobilization and 21/59 (35.6%) patients with a lower limb injury did not receive a walking aid prescription. A total of 61 patients (36.7%) had an absent or inadequate referral diagnosis. Factors associated with lower quality depended on the specific quality indicator and included: living further away from the hospital, younger age, initially consulting at another health care center, having a fracture, and being a smoker."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The high frequency of low quality of care underlines the necessity for orthopedic surgeons to be involved in primary care education. Identifying factors associated with lower quality of care will orient efforts to improve medical care of patients with isolated traumatic injury."
        }
      ]},
